# fstab — HackiOS-begonia patched variant (iPhone10,3 · iOS 16.5 · Build 20F66)
#
# Installed by ../apply_rootfs_patches.sh step (b) as /private/etc/fstab,
# with the pristine stock copy preserved at /private/etc/fstab.hackios-orig.
#
# Stock iPhone10,3 (iOS 16.5) fstab is a single-line-style pair, system volume
# read-only:
#
#     /dev/disk0s1s1	/			apfs	ro		0	0
#     /dev/disk0s2	/private/var		apfs	rw		0	2
#
# The IMMUTABILITY of stock / comes from (1) the boot chain mounting the system
# volume from a (battery-era snapshot of the) sealed volume read-only, and
# (2) Signed System Volume enforcement (iOS 15+, enforced on A11). Our port
# neither enforces a seal nor uses a snapshot, so the difference between this
# file and stock is the flag `rw` — see userland/README.md §4.
#
# IMPORTANT: the device paths below MUST match what our XNU IOMedia stack
# publishes (UFS via 'ufshcd-mtk' @ 0x11270000 for disk0; GPT scheme per the
# plan §Phase 4). If bring-up ends up naming volumes differently, change the
# LEFT column ONLY — the mount FLAGS have to stay semantically identical.

/dev/disk0s1s1	/			apfs	rw		0	0
/dev/disk0s2	/private/var		apfs	rw	nosuid	0	2

# Our XNU port boots the root volume WITHOUT an APFS snapshot (no SSV seal to
# mount). If a bring-up build exposes the raw volume (no snapshot index), use:
#
#    /dev/disk0s1	/		apfs	rw		0	0
#
# M4 test ramdisk (HFS+ memory disk built by ../ramdisk/mkramdisk.sh). Never
# inserted into the real fstab — it is referenced by boot args (rd=md0):
#
#    /dev/md0		/			hfs	rw		0	1
